{:edb/db-before-t 2207 :edb/db-after-t 2208 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535520 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:44:01.197Z" 13194139535520 true] [17592186068525 1100 "Find the points of intersection, if any, of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1$ and the line $y=-x+3$." 13194139535520 false] [17592186068525 1100 "Find the points of intersection, if any, of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1$ and the line $y=-x+3.$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186068525 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x-2}+1 \\\\ y=-x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2}+1 & =-x+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-2} & =-x+2 \\\\ 1 & =(-x+2)(x-2) \\\\ 1 & =-{x}^{2}+4x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16-20}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/30eb7faffa9cdfbffedbbf0c9d48408fb1be1b8101da80864c4bced5d53d9903.png)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186068525 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{1}{x - 2} + 1; y =-x + 3\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x - 2} + 1 &= -x + 3 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{x - 2} &= -x + 2 \\\\\n1 &= (-x + 2)(x - 2) \\\\\n1 &= -x^{2} + 4x - 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (4 ± \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} - 4(1)(5)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{16 - 20}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+3\n$$\n\ndoes not intersect the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/c1d0bf837c05cf29a2460425213cd5d1.png)" 13194139535520 false] [17592186068526 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/76c0e8aed74f2dcf3a382f79768098eda4f359631cdffb812a0aeccbb9707976.png)\n\nFind the points where the line $y=2x-3$ and the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}$ intersect (shown as the points $A$ and $B$ above)." 13194139535520 true] [17592186068526 1100 "Find the points where the line $y=2x-3$ and the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}$ intersect (shown as the points $A$ and $B$ above).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/314995c0a1ee3ffb68009d42ba923120.png)" 13194139535520 false] [17592186068526 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x-3 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ (x-2)(2x-3) & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+6 & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(2x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(1)-3 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1).$\n\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5}{2})-3 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186068526 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = 2x - 3; y = \\frac{1}{x - 2}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 3 &= \\frac{1}{x - 2} \\\\\n(x - 2)(2x - 3) &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 7x + 6 &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 7x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1, \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1$, we get\n$y|= 2x - 3; = 2(1) - 3; =-1$,\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1)$.\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}$, we get\n$y|= 2x - 3; = 2(\\frac{5}{2}) - 3; = 2$,\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right)\n$$" 13194139535520 false] [17592186068530 1100 "The line $y=2x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}$, where $k \\neq 0$. What is the range of possible values of $k$?" 13194139535520 false] [17592186068530 1100 "The line $y=2x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}$, where $k \\neq 0$. What is the range of possible values of $k?$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186068530 1145 "To determine whether the line\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$\n\nintersects the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}\n$$\n\nwe need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{k}{x + 3}; y = 2x + 3\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{k}{x + 3} &= 2x + 3 \\\\\nk &= (2x + 3)(x + 3) \\\\\nk &= 2x^{2} + 9x + 9 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 9x + 9 - k &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (-9 ± \\sqrt{9^{2} - 4(2)(9 - k)})/(2(2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9 ± \\sqrt{81 - 72 + 8k}}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9 ± \\sqrt{9 + 8k}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the line *not* to intersect the reciprocal function, the quadratic formula above must *not* give any real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 + 8k & < 0 \\\\ 8k & < - 9 \\\\ k & < - \\frac{9}{8}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is\n\n$$\nk<-\\dfrac{9}{8}\n$$" 13194139535520 false] [17592186068530 1145 "To determine whether the line $y=2x+3$ intersects the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3},$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{k}{x+3} \\\\ y=2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{x+3} & =2x+3 \\\\ k & =(2x+3)(x+3) \\\\ k & =2{x}^{2}+9x+9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+9x+9-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{{9}^{2}-4(2)(9-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{81-72+8k}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{9+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the line *not* to intersect the reciprocal function, the quadratic formula above must *not* give any real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+8k & <0 \\\\ 8k & <-9 \\\\ k & <-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-\\dfrac{9}{8}.$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186068812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353400 13194139535520 true] [17592186068812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353421 13194139535520 true] [17592186068812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353426 13194139535520 true] [17592186068812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353435 13194139535520 true] [17592186068813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353449 13194139535520 true] [17592186068813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353455 13194139535520 true] [17592186068813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353467 13194139535520 true] [17592186068813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353474 13194139535520 true] [17592186068814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353407 13194139535520 true] [17592186068814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353414 13194139535520 true] [17592186068814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353445 13194139535520 true] [17592186068814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353460 13194139535520 true] [17592186069194 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535520 true] [17592186069194 1100 "The curve $y = \\frac{1}{x}$ intersects the line $4y - 5x = 8$ at the points $(p, q)$ and $(r, s)$. What is $p + q + r + s$?" 13194139535520 false] [17592186069194 1100 "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ intersects the line $4y-5x=8$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186069194 1145 "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186069256 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535520 true] [17592186069256 1100 "If the line $y =-2x + 3$ and the curve $y = \\frac{2}{x - 2} + 1$ intersect at the point $(p, q)$, then $p + q =$" 13194139535520 false] [17592186069256 1100 "If the line $y=-2x+3$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186069256 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186069258 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535520 true] [17592186069258 1100 "The line $y = 2x + 4$ intersects the hyperbola $y =-\\frac{k + 1}{x} + 1$, where $k \\ne - 1$, only once. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139535520 false] [17592186069258 1100 "The line $y=2x+4$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1$, where $k\\ne -1$, only once. What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186069258 1145 "To determine whether the line $y=2x+4$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1$,  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x+4 &  \\\\ y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1 \\\\ 2x+3 & =-\\frac{k+1}{x} \\\\ (2x+3)x & =-(k+1) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3x+k+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(2)(k+1)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-8k-8}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{1-8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=\\frac{1}{8}.$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353400 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353401 13194139535520 true] [17592186353400 1089 "q-124047" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353400 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535520 true] [17592186353400 1100 "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}-1$ intersects the line $y=2x$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353400 1113 #uuid "8dbca007-d899-52ce-a942-d342784675f8" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353400 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x}-1 \\\\ y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}-1 & =2x \\\\ \\frac{1-x}{x} & =2x \\\\ 1-x & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-1,-2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$ \n\nFinally, if we let $p=-1,q=-2,r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $s=1,$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-1-2+\\frac{1}{2}+1=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/0a2420b1e390edd61efd28a223cd1beb93db98609a8d31c69526434e7188658c.png)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353402 13194139535520 true] [17592186353401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353403 13194139535520 true] [17592186353401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353404 13194139535520 true] [17592186353401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353405 13194139535520 true] [17592186353401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353406 13194139535520 true] [17592186353401 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535520 true] [17592186353401 1041 "selection" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353401 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353404 13194139535520 true] [17592186353401 1110 #uuid "43db9e13-58ca-5cf2-bb56-368b7812fa4f" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353402 1034 "-\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353402 1111 #uuid "7b44763a-e684-562b-bbe0-5ce48508660f" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353403 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353403 1111 #uuid "5e583496-bf8a-589a-b156-0fa2450297f3" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353404 1034 "-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353404 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353404 1111 #uuid "96c40755-cb82-5adc-ad34-21e752738c9f" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353405 1034 "-\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353405 1111 #uuid "fe9d5f04-670e-581f-a517-caf7ef897266" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353406 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353406 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353406 1111 #uuid "f58e94f1-162c-567d-92c4-3dc0d8303ed1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353407 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353408 13194139535520 true] [17592186353407 1089 "q-124166" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353407 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535520 true] [17592186353407 1100 "The line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$ for $k\\ne 0,$ only once. What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353407 1113 #uuid "d6311c5b-8c9a-5c5e-a71d-8fa36c325ce7" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353407 1145 "To determine whether the line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ 2x(x-2) & =k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(2)(-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =-16 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-2.$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353409 13194139535520 true] [17592186353408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353410 13194139535520 true] [17592186353408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353411 13194139535520 true] [17592186353408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353412 13194139535520 true] [17592186353408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353413 13194139535520 true] [17592186353408 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535520 true] [17592186353408 1041 "selection" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353408 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353413 13194139535520 true] [17592186353408 1110 #uuid "991d1ef8-82d2-5e01-8d23-22d16d6cca08" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353409 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353409 1111 #uuid "2865378e-353b-5726-90ac-e4d3575317a2" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353410 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353410 1111 #uuid "48c48589-8aa8-5528-8614-ddd34d0a608d" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353411 1034 "2" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353411 1111 #uuid "e3e0964a-a117-55ff-98d6-c1e8d26996bf" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353412 1034 "-4" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353412 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353412 1111 #uuid "bc0a7f8c-2c3f-5c83-ad31-694028d51cf4" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353413 1034 "-2" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353413 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353413 1111 #uuid "f9d804b2-7657-57ae-aa50-df1a72da6569" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353414 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353415 13194139535520 true] [17592186353414 1089 "q-124169" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353414 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535520 true] [17592186353414 1100 "The line $y=x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x}-k,$ where $k\\ne 0,$ twice. What is the range of possible values of  $k?$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353414 1113 #uuid "e0bd9754-4558-5a5b-aa2c-1054b22635af" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353414 1145 "To determine whether the line $y=x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x}-k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-k &  \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-k & =\\frac{k}{x}-k \\\\ x-k & =\\frac{k-kx}{x} \\\\ x(x-k) & =k-kx \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola at two points, the quadratic formula above must give two real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be positive: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k\\in (0,\\infty ).$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353416 13194139535520 true] [17592186353415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353417 13194139535520 true] [17592186353415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353418 13194139535520 true] [17592186353415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353419 13194139535520 true] [17592186353415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353420 13194139535520 true] [17592186353415 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535520 true] [17592186353415 1041 "selection" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353415 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353416 13194139535520 true] [17592186353415 1110 #uuid "0fd38efc-8f47-570b-918d-740240f15546" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353416 1034 "k\\in (0,\\infty )" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353416 1111 #uuid "b981ece2-4b8d-5566-9b5c-1ecae653988b" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353417 1034 "k\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353417 1111 #uuid "e55f9e77-30a5-5d0b-98c6-64a45b689dab" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353418 1034 "k\\in (0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353418 1111 #uuid "5d65e5eb-5529-5a3d-a08d-69ac6925baf3" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353419 1034 "k\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353419 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353419 1111 #uuid "fcd3fd2e-d47b-5113-bf47-d3c0ff57b358" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353420 1034 "k\\in (-\\infty ,0)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353420 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353420 1111 #uuid "a88d3f8e-a87e-5f24-be5a-eb3c1a0ebc60" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353421 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353422 13194139535520 true] [17592186353421 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353424 13194139535520 true] [17592186353421 1089 "q-276446" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353421 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535520 true] [17592186353421 1100 "The line $y=8x-1$ intersects the curve $y=\\frac{1}{2x}-1$ at the points $A(-\\frac{1}{4},-3)$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $B.$\n\nThe coordinates of $B$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353421 1113 #uuid "ff2d82e1-d4ad-5440-8ae8-351977e4748a" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353421 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=8x-1 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2x}-1 \\\\ 8x & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{1}{4})-1 \\\\  & =-2-1 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $A(-\\frac{1}{4},-3)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/bb69eb0150879f215fe453e596ae18273ec149f1d1486c8d7dfe7b2c1e3e5e58.png)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353422 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353423 13194139535520 true] [17592186353422 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353422 1041 "field-1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353422 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353423 13194139535520 true] [17592186353422 1110 #uuid "358b2618-25b0-5827-84e2-6ec7dee891ef" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353423 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353423 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353423 1111 #uuid "5e03ef29-2c0e-545d-91a3-8a8615adce26" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353424 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353425 13194139535520 true] [17592186353424 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353424 1041 "field-2" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353424 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353425 13194139535520 true] [17592186353424 1110 #uuid "e28d164b-210e-5e2d-b504-e8882b11a5b1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353425 1034 "1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353425 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353425 1111 #uuid "b6de156a-4d78-5f07-b94f-b4b088714e9a" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353426 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353427 13194139535520 true] [17592186353426 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353429 13194139535520 true] [17592186353426 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353431 13194139535520 true] [17592186353426 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353433 13194139535520 true] [17592186353426 1089 "q-276447" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353426 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535520 true] [17592186353426 1100 "**From left to right,** the points of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}-5$ and the line $y=8x+1$ are $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $A$ and $B.$\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}\n$),\\,B($\n{{field-3}},\n{{field-4}}\n$).$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353426 1113 #uuid "0cd54ca1-3fb2-5614-b9ee-7bc628e79ad6" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353426 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{x}-5 \\\\ y=8x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-5 & =8x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =8x+6 \\\\ 2 & =8{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+6x-2 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =0 \\\\ (4x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4},-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=-1.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-8+1 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(-1,-7).$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the points of intersection are $A(-1,-7)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},3).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/5432e52f6099ec6e2d72ea6031e303eeaa0043129eea40baa064dd59b0d46520.png)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353427 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353428 13194139535520 true] [17592186353427 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353427 1041 "field-1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353427 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353428 13194139535520 true] [17592186353427 1110 #uuid "0bcb652a-0264-5e74-b69c-daf5ebf7841d" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353428 1034 "-1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353428 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353428 1111 #uuid "9b94b8b2-c40f-531b-bf52-46e6824ecd3e" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353429 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353430 13194139535520 true] [17592186353429 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353429 1041 "field-2" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353429 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353430 13194139535520 true] [17592186353429 1110 #uuid "a198454d-f94c-570f-b43f-34acd0894009" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353430 1034 "-7" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353430 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353430 1111 #uuid "fff6902f-51c8-525b-89d0-0ea79086d519" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353431 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353432 13194139535520 true] [17592186353431 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353431 1041 "field-3" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353431 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353432 13194139535520 true] [17592186353431 1110 #uuid "dce7f94f-8cca-5b0c-8ab2-4fcbfa4126c4" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353432 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353432 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353432 1111 #uuid "db25c769-febc-5b75-8ce7-f7d80d6479d6" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353434 13194139535520 true] [17592186353433 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353433 1041 "field-4" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353433 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353434 13194139535520 true] [17592186353433 1110 #uuid "5d6f6e4e-cadd-512e-8bcb-16448b848b4a" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353434 1034 "3" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353434 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353434 1111 #uuid "210806d3-ad65-5137-9b3c-d1ffad23c002" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353436 13194139535520 true] [17592186353435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353439 13194139535520 true] [17592186353435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353441 13194139535520 true] [17592186353435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353443 13194139535520 true] [17592186353435 1089 "q-276450" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353435 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535520 true] [17592186353435 1100 "**From left to right,** the points of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{4}{x-1}+2$ and the line $y-x-1=0$ are $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $A$ and $B.$\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$),\\,B($\n{{field-3}},\n{{field-4}}$)$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353435 1113 #uuid "ac4ebe97-c33a-5aa8-8cba-aeb3f76762ab" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353435 1145 "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{4}{x-1}+2 \\\\ y=x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-1}+2 & =x+1 \\\\ 4+2(x-1) & =(x+1)(x-1) \\\\ 2+2x & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=3.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+1 \\\\  & =(-1)-1 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,0).$\n\n- Substituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+1 \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(3,4).$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the points of intersection are $A(-1,0)$ and $B(3,4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/6166b8bbae6ae1e528a72a5533e0e6906547e7e6076790cdb23306d11bfe9063.png)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353437 13194139535520 true] [17592186353436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353438 13194139535520 true] [17592186353436 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353436 1041 "field-1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353436 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353438 13194139535520 true] [17592186353436 1110 #uuid "65f70b1b-af72-59ee-bbed-98111ec3e8c0" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353437 1034 "1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353437 1111 #uuid "835dfcfb-621a-5788-aa01-6f7ff6ad44ff" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353438 1034 "-1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353438 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353438 1111 #uuid "a3eebeb8-c2a7-5bc4-a3e9-60afe939fadf" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353440 13194139535520 true] [17592186353439 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353439 1041 "field-2" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353439 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353440 13194139535520 true] [17592186353439 1110 #uuid "c7bcd62c-1aa6-55ca-a6ab-c2a495a02e16" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353440 1034 "0" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353440 1111 #uuid "694915b7-44e6-5667-b6e2-7aa1b9018f3f" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353441 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353442 13194139535520 true] [17592186353441 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353441 1041 "field-3" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353441 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353442 13194139535520 true] [17592186353441 1110 #uuid "4658616e-feae-543c-8e96-702868c6b930" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353442 1034 "3" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353442 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353442 1111 #uuid "fc38097e-3068-5bb3-93cc-03c373b036df" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353443 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353444 13194139535520 true] [17592186353443 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353443 1041 "field-4" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353443 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353444 13194139535520 true] [17592186353443 1110 #uuid "82ff66f3-2e39-52ec-96a7-3e6ba2d32d4a" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353444 1034 "4" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353444 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353444 1111 #uuid "ffa37356-8622-5608-9eb9-b59ac67bc765" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353445 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353446 13194139535520 true] [17592186353445 1089 "q-276454" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353445 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535520 true] [17592186353445 1100 "The line $y=4x-5$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k+5}{x}+1$, where $k\\ne -5,$  only once. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353445 1113 #uuid "12cfca36-2a8c-5aa4-9ec4-72deb70147e1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353445 1145 "To determine whether the line $y=4x-5$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k+5}{x}+1$,  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:      \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=4x-5 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{k+5}{x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-5 & =\\frac{k+5}{x}+1 \\\\ 4x-6 & =\\frac{k+5}{x} \\\\ (4x-6)x & =k+5 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6x-k-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-6)\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4(4)(-k-5)}}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{36+16k+80}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{116+16k}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}116+16k & =0 \\\\ 16k & =-116 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{116}{16} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{29\\cdot 4}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{29}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the value of $k$ is $-\\frac{29}{4}.$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353446 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353447 13194139535520 true] [17592186353446 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353448 13194139535520 true] [17592186353446 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353446 1041 "field-1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353446 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353447 13194139535520 true] [17592186353446 1110 #uuid "3e98764e-75b8-5961-a63c-2b3e568043d7" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353447 1034 "-\\frac{29}{4}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353447 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353447 1111 #uuid "bbeb9607-540e-5b38-81e2-dc83a3923c10" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353448 1034 "0" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353448 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353448 1111 #uuid "d49701b5-0844-503e-af4a-5a22e5c6953c" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353449 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353450 13194139535520 true] [17592186353449 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353449 1089 "q-277383" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353449 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535520 true] [17592186353449 1100 "Find the coordinates of $A$ which is the point of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2$ and the line $y=4.$\n\nThe coordinates of $A$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353449 1113 #uuid "32c28e87-d455-5d16-b110-f34103372724" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353449 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 & =4 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3x-2} & =2 \\\\ 5 & =6x-4 \\\\ 9 & =6x \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(\\frac{3}{2},4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/f07fc53462af6fbb7de0a901ae23721a2eb84f6e83bd5577a2659c175519fc67.png)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353450 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353451 13194139535520 true] [17592186353450 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353452 13194139535520 true] [17592186353450 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353450 1041 "field-1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353450 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353451 13194139535520 true] [17592186353450 1110 #uuid "6578b694-5677-5348-b9e2-f194845b88ce" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353451 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353451 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353451 1111 #uuid "b334e3ee-2bd7-56bc-95c6-e529c9335614" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353452 1034 "0" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353452 1111 #uuid "67c3f372-db60-5d3b-b0f6-3b7e4264b418" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353453 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353454 13194139535520 true] [17592186353453 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353453 1041 "field-2" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353453 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353454 13194139535520 true] [17592186353453 1110 #uuid "26473e1e-b44d-5c32-8de0-75c8c7170d45" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353454 1034 "4" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353454 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353454 1111 #uuid "08f804a2-2cb6-54f1-a44d-6095b1e6a53c" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353455 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353456 13194139535520 true] [17592186353455 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353458 13194139535520 true] [17592186353455 1089 "q-277386" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353455 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535520 true] [17592186353455 1100 "Find the coordinates of $A$ which is the point of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{2x+3}$ and the line $y=1.$\n\nThe coordinates of $A$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353455 1113 #uuid "b11e759f-ff0f-5f2b-84fc-097bfa970897" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353455 1145 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{2x+3} \\\\ y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2x+3} & =1 \\\\ 2 & =2x+3 \\\\ -1 & =2x \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/192f47788ac2bc716c4f3e0d8fa9efe9b2c26dd9e32bb9296cfcb00328e5db05.png)" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353456 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353457 13194139535520 true] [17592186353456 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353456 1041 "field-1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353456 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353457 13194139535520 true] [17592186353456 1110 #uuid "7e55454a-0b79-5ea6-b864-fb6936097e11" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353457 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353457 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353457 1111 #uuid "16a0bdb8-f5bb-54ba-b852-975fdb1373f2" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353459 13194139535520 true] [17592186353458 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535520 true] [17592186353458 1041 "field-2" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353458 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353459 13194139535520 true] [17592186353458 1110 #uuid "45e7b644-0a0a-53d2-bfff-ff46b0c942e0" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353459 1034 "1" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353459 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535520 true] [17592186353459 1111 #uuid "dd56dcc3-0b21-55f2-ba30-ce3a18d41dbd" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353460 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353461 13194139535520 true] [17592186353460 1089 "q-277432" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353460 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535520 true] [17592186353460 1100 "The line $y=4x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{2}{x+1}$ only once. What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353460 1113 #uuid "17f6bb74-ea59-5538-b3d8-73cf1622119d" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353460 1145 "To determine whether the line $y=4x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{2}{x+1},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=4x-k \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-k & =-\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ (4x-k)(x+1) & =-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+4x-kx-k+2 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+(4-k)x+(2-k) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(4-k)\\pm \\sqrt{(4-k)^{2}-4(4)(2-k)}}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-4)\\pm \\sqrt{16-8k+{k}^{2}-32+16k}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-4)\\pm \\sqrt{{k}^{2}+8k-16}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+8k-16 & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{(8)^{2}-4(1)(-16)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64+64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm 8\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\pm 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-4-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $k=-4+4\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353462 13194139535520 true] [17592186353461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353463 13194139535520 true] [17592186353461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353464 13194139535520 true] [17592186353461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353465 13194139535520 true] [17592186353461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353466 13194139535520 true] [17592186353461 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535520 true] [17592186353461 1041 "selection" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353461 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353465 13194139535520 true] [17592186353461 1110 #uuid "e712c2e3-9e1c-57a9-bfbd-3a15d1f17767" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353462 1034 "$-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $4\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535520 true] [17592186353462 1111 #uuid "948c1111-14bb-55ba-87ff-a3dc7dd4cefb" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353463 1034 "$-\\sqrt{2}$ or $\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535520 true] [17592186353463 1111 #uuid "d8181938-1fad-56b0-b232-980f7396e09c" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353464 1034 "$-8-8\\sqrt{2}$ or $-8+8\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535520 true] [17592186353464 1111 #uuid "81ba21df-f1e3-5d0f-a1cc-816516151648" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353465 1034 "$-4-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $-4+4\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535520 true] [17592186353465 1111 #uuid "0a168de5-5b03-5edf-a2f6-178a101dbfbf" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353466 1034 "$-2-2\\sqrt{2}$ or $-2+2\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535520 true] [17592186353466 1111 #uuid "81858d3a-ca21-54d5-bc98-af2680576a7d" 13194139535520 true] [17592186353467 1017 #edb/ref 175921